% A class function's constructor-arity errors belong to no checking
% unit — and are still reported.
%
% A `:- class` function's equations are checked in throwaway
% per-signature sub-sessions whose diagnostics are dropped, so their
% constructor-arity errors are collected at program level instead,
% outside any unit, where they suppress no warnings. That is the
% "no unit" arm of the equation walk (`funs_arity_errors` in
% `typechecker2/tc2_pure.chr`), and nothing else in the corpus
% reaches it: `typecheck_fun_equation_units` is the one other directory
% pairing `:- class` with a wrong-arity constructor, and there the bad
% use sits in a plain function, so the class function's own walk
% returns nothing.
%
% `cf`'s equation applies `b/1` at arity 2. Both halves matter: the
% error has to survive the sub-session that discards the equation's
% other diagnostics, and it has to stay out of unit 0, which belongs
% to the rule below.
:- module(typecheck_class_fun_ctor_arity, [go/1]).
:- use_module(library(prelude)).

:- chr_type box ---> b(int).
:- chr_constraint go(box).

:- class (cf(int) -> box), (cf(string) -> box).
cf(_) -> b(1, 2).

go(R) <=> R is cf(1).
